## Raw verdict

### Verdict: fail

1. (major) No darkness/contrast — pervasive yellow-green haze flattens the
   whole frame vs. reference's near-black/high-contrast look.
2. (major) Sconces don't read as light sources — flat yellow cone icons, no
   visible falloff on adjacent walls.
3. (major) Wall material reads as flat repeating placeholder tile, not wood
   wainscot; no visible parallax/normal depth.
4. (minor) Rug texture appears to bleed onto the foreground railing/landing.
5. (minor) Unexplained green glow at the corridor's vanishing point.
6. (minor) Railings read as a dock/bridge, not manor wainscoting.

## Investigation / disposition (post-hoc, by task-10 agent)

- **Issues 1–3 (haze/contrast, sconces not reading as lights, flat walls) —
  test-harness lighting artifact, not a manor-overhaul defect.**
  `test/ArenaConnTest.cs` lines 112–119 hard-codes its own `WorldEnvironment`
  with `AmbientLightColor(0.40,0.40,0.40)` / `AmbientLightEnergy=1.0` for QA
  visibility. Real gameplay corridors (built via the same `ArenaBuilder`/
  `DungeonBuilder`, rendered inside `scenes/OnlineDungeonArena.cs`) use
  `AmbientLightColor(0.10,0.07,0.04)` / `AmbientLightEnergy=0.25` — a
  deliberate near-black art-direction target per `MEMORY.md`. The actual
  torches (`scripts/DungeonBuilder.cs:1280-1291`, warm color
  `(1.0,0.68,0.22)`, `LightEnergy=2.8`, `OmniRange=14`) are real
  `OmniLight3D`s with meaningful energy/range — they simply can't read as
  distinct light pools when the harness's own flat ambient is already 4x
  brighter than gameplay. **Escalating (potential follow-up: a
  "gameplay-lit" ArenaConnTest capture variant that reuses
  `OnlineDungeonArena`'s ambient settings instead of its own bright QA
  environment), not fixing in this task** — changing the shared QA harness's
  ambient risks hurting visibility for future geometry/placement QA, and
  wall-texture material work is out of this task's scope.
- **Issue 5 (green glow at corridor end) — misread, not a bug.** Checked the
  adjacent frame (`qa_corridor00000002.png`): the "green glow" is the
  arena's grass floor and a green swag/pillar accent visible through the
  archway at the end of the bridge — legitimate distant geometry, not a
  stray light or placeholder. Verified the only colored point lights in
  `DungeonBuilder.cs` are warm torches `(1.0,0.68,0.22)` and a red exit glow
  `(1.0,0.10,0.08)` — no green light source exists in the codebase. No fix
  needed.
- **Issue 4 (rug bleeding onto railing) — plausible, but touches a
  documented fragile area.** The rounded rug/floor patch sits right at the
  arena-connection landing (bridge meets archway). CLAUDE.md explicitly
  lists "Center arena stitching between generated maps and handmade maps"
  and "Openings between maze pieces" as **known fragile areas** — not
  something to touch with an ad-hoc QA-task fix. Recorded as an escalation
  for a dedicated look, not attempted here.
- **Issue 6 (railings read as dock/bridge) — subjective/style note,
  minor.** No code defect identified; ships as-is per godogen rules (minor
  = ship).

## Disposition

**Escalated to controller** — issues 1–3 are dominated by the QA harness's
own (deliberately brighter) ambient lighting rather than the manor
overhaul's materials/geometry; issue 4 touches a documented fragile area
(arena stitching) that's out of this task's minimal-fix scope; issue 5 is a
misread; issue 6 is a minor/ship-as-is style note. No code changes made as a
result of this verdict.

